// F19 -- the ###Connection section accepts an element with NO closing brace.
//
// Run:
//   java -cp <engine-runner>/target/classes:$(cat cp.txt) perf.ParseMain model.pure
//
// Observed: ok -- accepted.
// Expected: a parse error naming the unbalanced brace.
//
// The RelationalDatabaseConnection below is never closed. The ###Pure section that follows
// is still parsed correctly (the PMCD contains both `connection fx::C` and `class fx::A`),
// so nothing is silently swallowed -- the parser simply does not require the brace.
//
// Section-specific. The same mutation is REJECTED in ###Pure, ###Relational, ###Service and
// ###Diagram:
//     ###Pure       Class fx::A { x: String[1];        -> Unexpected token
//     ###Relational Database fx::D ( Table T (...)     -> Unexpected token
//     ###Service    Service fx::S { pattern: '/x';     -> Unexpected token
//     ###Diagram    Diagram fx::G { classView v {...}  -> Unexpected token
// so this is not a general end-of-input leniency; it is the Connection grammar alone.
//
// Found by the mutation harness (scripts/parser/mutants.py), which drops the final
// delimiter of every fixture: 16 of 51 fixtures tolerated it and ALL 16 were ###Connection.
// A single hand-written negative would most likely have been written against ###Pure and
// found nothing.
###Connection
RelationalDatabaseConnection fx::C
{
   store: fx::Db;
   type: H2;
   specification: LocalH2 { };
   auth: DefaultH2;

###Pure
Class fx::A
{
   x: String[1];
}
